Criteria
(9)
Appointees will be leading practitioners in their professional fields who must be able to contribute in a translational capacity to the teaching and research efforts of the University by translating research, or the pedagogy of research, into practice in the broad public sector.
(10)
Criteria for appointment:
Level E appointees normally would be leading proponents with an international reputation;
Level D appointees would be leading proponents at least at national level;
Must have recognised and demonstrable level of competence as judged by their peers and be leading practice in elite settings;
Appointees would not necessarily have a PhD but normally would have a Masters qualification;
Evidence of research productivity, which may differ from that expected for a standard academic appointment, but esteem factors would be high;
Must be practising at the time of appointment and continue to practice and maintain registration while at the University;
If retired, they should be moved to another category, such as adjunct appointments.

Terms of Appointment
(12)
Appointments will be:
Remunerated at the respective salary rates for Level D or Level E academics;
Fractional, normally 0.4 to 0.6 FTE;
Fixed term for two years and renewable.

Process
(13)
The Provost will submit a proposal to the Vice-Chancellor (for Level E) or to the Chief People Officer (for Level D).
(14)
The proposal will include:
A rationale for making such an appointment and what the expectations for the role will be;
Job card and position description;
Funding source, internal or external;
a copy of the proposed appointee’s curriculum vitae;
the names and contact details of three disciplinary experts who could attest to the proposed appointee’s national/international standing and their effectiveness in translating research into practice;
Proposed selection committee membership (refer to section below).
(15)
If the proposal is supported by the Vice-Chancellor or the Chief People Officer (respectively), a selection committee will be appointed.
(16)
The selection process will be similar to that for a standard Level E or Level D appointment (refer to standard selection procedures for relevant level). Normally this would entail interviewing the candidate.
